# Project Parallax — Build Blueprint

## 1. Project Summary

Project Parallax is a director-defined adaptive cinema application.

A filmmaker provides:

- a canonical short film;
- a set of explicitly marked adaptive slots;
- constraints describing what may and may not change;
- a canonical fallback clip for every slot.

A viewer selects a profile containing location and language preferences.

The application then:

1. loads the canonical film;
2. checks whether personalized assets already exist for each adaptive slot;
3. generates missing image assets with `gemini-3.1-flash-lite-image`;
4. generates or edits short video inserts with `gemini-omni-flash-preview`;
5. validates and caches successful outputs;
6. plays personalized clips when ready;
7. plays the canonical fallback when generation is unavailable or misses its deadline.

The hackathon demo should use a 45–90 second original short film with exactly three adaptive slots.

---

## 2. Primary Demo Goal

Demonstrate that:

- the director controls which parts of the movie may change;
- the same story can be rendered differently for different viewers;
- location and language personalization can happen inside the cinematic world;
- generated assets are prepared ahead of playback;
- playback never blocks on generation;
- a canonical fallback always exists;
- cached cuts can be replayed immediately;
- three personalized cuts can be compared side by side.

The application should feel like a prototype of a new film format, not merely a prompt-to-video interface.

---

## 3. Codename

**Project Parallax**

Tagline:

> One story. A different world for every viewer.

Product descriptor:

> Director-authored adaptive cinema.

---

## 4. Scope

### In scope

- Web application
- Director Studio
- Viewer profile selector
- Adaptive video player
- Three adaptive slot types
- Location personalization
- Language personalization
- Nano Banana keyframe and prop generation
- Gemini Omni short video generation or editing
- Pre-generation
- Cache lookup
- Canonical fallback
- Generation status UI
- Side-by-side comparison
- Simulated and real generation modes
- Manual approval for generated assets
- Lightweight validation
- Demo-ready seed data

### Out of scope

- Full non-linear video editor
- Arbitrary commercial movie ingestion
- Celebrity or actor replacement
- Real-time lip-sync replacement
- Voice cloning
- Full account and authentication system
- Payment or ad marketplace
- Production-grade DRM
- Long-form film generation
- Automatic demographic inference
- Frame-perfect transitions across arbitrary shots
- Fully automated moderation pipeline

---

## 5. Recommended Technology Stack

Use a stack optimized for speed of implementation.

### Frontend

- Next.js 15+
- TypeScript
- React
- Tailwind CSS
- ShadCN UI
- HTML5 video
- HLS.js only if needed
- Zustand for client state
- React Query or native server actions for API state

### Backend

Use one of:

- Next.js route handlers for the hackathon; or
- FastAPI if Antigravity prefers a separate backend.

Preferred for speed:

- Next.js route handlers
- Background job abstraction implemented in-process initially
- Firebase or Supabase for metadata
- Cloud Storage, Firebase Storage, or local filesystem for media

### Storage

For the fastest implementation:

- local filesystem in development;
- Firebase Storage or Google Cloud Storage in deployed mode.

### Database

Use Firestore or Supabase Postgres.

Recommended collections/tables:

- movies
- slots
- viewer_profiles
- generation_jobs
- generated_assets
- cache_entries

### AI models

- Image generation and editing: `gemini-3.1-flash-lite-image`
- Short video generation and editing: `gemini-omni-flash-preview`

All model calls must be wrapped behind server-side service interfaces.

