## 13. Prompt Templates

Keep prompts simple and constrained.

### City keyframe prompt

```text
Create a cinematic 16:9 establishing frame for a mystery film.

Location: {{city}}, {{country}}
Viewer language and signage locale: {{locale}}
Time: night
Mood: quiet, cinematic, slightly mysterious
Camera: wide establishing shot
Weather: subtle and realistic
Visual style: grounded contemporary cinema
Duration target for later animation: 4 seconds

Include:
- subtle local architectural cues;
- one or two readable signs in the requested language;
- realistic street lighting;
- no famous person;
- no brand logos;
- no text other than natural environmental signage.

The shot must work as an insert between two existing film cuts.
```

### City animation prompt

```text
Animate this image into a {{duration}} second cinematic establishing shot.

Use:
- slow camera drift;
- subtle vehicle and environmental movement;
- realistic light reflections;
- natural ambient sound;
- no new foreground subject;
- no change to the city identity;
- no change to the signage language.

Keep the composition, visual identity, lighting, and mood the same.
```

### Localized newspaper prompt

```text
Create a realistic newspaper front page used as a clue in a mystery film.

Language: {{languageLabel}}
Locale: {{locale}}
City: {{city}}

Required narrative facts:
{{immutableFacts}}

Editable details:
{{editableFields}}

Requirements:
- cinematic realism;
- readable main headline;
- correct language;
- no real political claims;
- no real person accused of wrongdoing;
- fictional publication name;
- clear warning symbol;
- suitable for a 16:9 close-up insert;
- preserve empty margins for safe video cropping.
```

### TV bulletin prompt

```text
Create a fictional local television news graphic for a mystery film.

Language: {{languageLabel}}
City: {{city}}
Country: {{country}}

Required story fact:
{{immutableFacts}}

Requirements:
- fictional channel;
- readable lower-third;
- restrained broadcast design;
- no real breaking-news claims;
- no celebrity or public figure;
- no real company logo;
- composition safe for animation and video insertion.
```

### Omni edit prompt

```text
Modify only the adaptive element described below.

Adaptive element:
{{slot.label}}

Viewer context:
- City: {{city}}
- Country: {{country}}
- Locale: {{locale}}

Required facts:
{{immutableFacts}}

Editable fields:
{{editableFields}}

Preserve:
- all characters;
- camera timing;
- shot duration;
- lighting;
- foreground action;
- audio unless the slot explicitly allows language changes;
- every non-adaptive object.

Keep everything else the same.
```

---

## 14. Playback Architecture

Do not attempt one dynamically modified video file at first.

Build the movie as a sequence of canonical and adaptive clips.

Example:

```ts
export interface PlaybackSegment {
  id: string;
  type: "CANONICAL" | "ADAPTIVE";
  startSeconds: number;
  endSeconds: number;
  canonicalUrl: string;
  personalizedUrl?: string;
  activeUrl: string;
  status: "READY" | "GENERATING" | "FALLBACK";
}
```

Seed sequence:

```text
canonical-01.mp4
slot-city.mp4
canonical-02.mp4
slot-newspaper.mp4
canonical-03.mp4
slot-tv.mp4
canonical-04.mp4
```

This is more reliable than manipulating one long video during playback.

### Player behavior

Before playback:

1. Resolve all segments.
2. Replace adaptive segment URL with personalized URL if ready.
3. Otherwise use canonical fallback.
4. Preload current and next segment.
5. Begin playback immediately.

During playback:

- never pause for generation;
- periodically re-check not-yet-played slots;
- swap in a generated asset only if it is ready before preload begins;
- once a segment is preloaded, do not change it mid-playback;
- record whether personalized or fallback footage was shown.

### Simplest implementation

Use one `<video>` element and switch source at segment boundaries.

Requirements:

- preload next clip;
- preserve continuous playback;
- hide loading spinner unless playback truly stalls;
- optional 150–250 ms crossfade or black-frame transition;
- synchronize cumulative timeline manually.

Alternative:

- concatenate approved clips server-side with FFmpeg before playback.

Recommended hackathon path:

- use segmented playback first;
- optionally add a “Render Final Cut” action with FFmpeg later.

---

## 15. Look-Ahead Scheduler

Generation should start before playback reaches a slot.

Pseudo-code:

```ts
async function prepareMovieCut(
  movie: Movie,
  slots: AdaptiveSlot[],
  profile: ViewerProfile
) {
  for (const slot of slots) {
    const cached = await findApprovedCachedAsset(movie, slot, profile);

    if (cached) {
      markSlotReady(slot.id, cached.assetUrl, "CACHE_HIT");
      continue;
    }

    markSlotGenerating(slot.id);
    enqueueGenerationJob(movie, slot, profile);
  }
}
```

During playback:

```ts
function resolveSlotAsset(slotState: SlotState): string {
  if (
    slotState.status === "READY" &&
    slotState.validationPassed &&
    slotState.approved &&
    slotState.personalizedAssetUrl
  ) {
    return slotState.personalizedAssetUrl;
  }

  return slotState.canonicalFallbackUrl;
}
```

Deadline rule:

```ts
if (currentPlaybackSeconds >= slot.startSeconds - slot.generationDeadlineSeconds) {
  lockSlotForThisPlayback(slot.id);
}
```

After a slot is locked:

- late generation may still be cached for future playback;
- it must not replace the currently scheduled fallback.

---

## 16. Validation

Validation must be lightweight and layered.

### Deterministic checks

- file exists;
- file can be opened;
- image dimensions are valid;
- video duration is within tolerance;
- aspect ratio is expected;
- codec is playable;
- file size is non-zero;
- generated output is not identical to an error image.

### Model-assisted checks

Ask a multimodal Gemini model to inspect:

- location match;
- language match;
- required story facts;
- prohibited elements;
- visual continuity;
- whether the slot remains faithful to the narrative purpose.

### Manual approval

Director Studio must support:

- approve;
- reject;
- regenerate;
- use fallback;
- add a note.

For the live demo, pre-approve primary profiles.

---

## 17. Director Studio UI

### Main layout

Desktop-first.

```text
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Project Parallax / Movie Title                           │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Video Preview                                            │
│                                                          │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Timeline: [canonical][slot1][canonical][slot2][slot3]    │
├──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┤
│ Slot Inspector               │ Generated Variants        │
│                              │                           │
│ Type                         │ Visakhapatnam / Telugu     │
│ Start / End                  │ Tokyo / Japanese          │
│ Editable fields              │ San Francisco / English   │
│ Immutable facts              │                           │
│ Constraints                  │ Approve / Reject          │
│ Fallback                     │ Regenerate                 │
└──────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┘
```

### Required interactions

- click a slot marker;
- view slot metadata;
- edit constraints;
- preview fallback;
- select a test profile;
- generate;
- inspect job state;
- approve/reject;
- view cache key;
- preview final slot.

Do not implement drag-and-drop timeline editing unless time remains.

